-- #1182 SPECIFICITY PROBE — the discriminator that identifies the MECHANISM.
--
-- ⚠️ This file is NOT run by the must-fail gate. It is shipped because it answers the one
-- question main.mdk + control.mdk cannot: is the winner picked by DECLARATION ORDER, or by
-- the most-specific-wins (min⊑) selector ranking impls of two DIFFERENT interfaces against
-- each other in one candidate set?
--
-- Here `impl A2 (Q x y z)` is fully general and declared FIRST; `impl A1 (Q Int Bool Int)`
-- is strictly more specific and declared SECOND. If declaration order decided, the answer
-- would be 2. It is 1 — the more specific impl, from the OTHER interface, whose method name
-- is not even bound in this file. Only cross-interface min⊑ ranking explains that.
--
-- Re-posed onto the cross-module axis with the rest of this fixture (2026-08-15); the
-- mechanism SURVIVED the move unchanged, which is itself the finding — the candidate-set
-- deformation is not a single-file artifact.
--
-- Re-derive in one command (measured 2026-08-15) rather than trusting this note:
--   medaka run specificity-probe.mdk   -> 1, exit 0
import a1.{A1, m}
import a2.{A2}

data Q a b c = Q a b c

impl A2 (Q x y z) where
  m _ = 2

impl A1 (Q Int Bool Int) where
  m _ = 1

main = println (m (Q 1 True 7))
